-- title:
--   Orbit centre generates the Drinfeld chart witness ideal
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. Let $q$ be a prime and $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero, $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity, and assume (`hι`) that there is a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = e^{2\pi i/q}$. Let $H_1$ be a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ equal to $\mathrm{levelH}(q,M') \cap \ker\big((\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times\big)$, where $\mathrm{levelH}(q,M')$ is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$; thus $H_1$ consists of the units that are $\equiv 1$ both modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((T))$ equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the subfield of $L((T))$ generated over $L$ by the coefficientwise image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the field of $q$-expansions attached to $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ over $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   Local base and chart. Let $A$ be a Henselian local discrete valuation ring which is a domain with fraction field $L$ and with algebraically closed residue field, such that $q$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$; $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Let $j \in K$ be nonzero with Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion $T^{-1} \cdot (\text{integral } j\text{-numerator})$. Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A (↥K) j` for the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and let $X :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A (↥K) j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) be the two-chart integral model, the pushout of $\mathrm{Spec}\,C \leftarrow \mathrm{Spec}(\text{middle algebra}) \rightarrow \mathrm{Spec}\,C_\infty$, with structure morphism `toBase` to $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$ and open immersion `ιFin` from $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$. Let $\varpi \in A$ generate the maximal ideal of $A$, and let $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfy $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$.
--
--   The supersingular point. Let $y$ be a maximal ideal of $C$ containing the image of $\varpi$, and assume (`hss`) that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$ the element $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of $j_0 \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $j_0$ has no nonzero affine point killed by $q$. Let $z$ be a point of $X$ and $\varpi_z$ the germ at $z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under `toBase`, assumed to lie in the maximal ideal of the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$. Let $y'$ be a point of $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$ with `ιFin` image $z$, satisfying the same supersingularity condition (`hss'`) for the prime $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, and assume $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal} = y$.
--
--   The local model. Let $W_1$ be a domain which is a discrete valuation ring, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, and $\sigma_1 : A \to W_1$ a ring homomorphism with $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1} = (\sigma_1\varpi)$. Let $f_1, u_1, v_1 \in W_1[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u_1, v_1$ units and $f_1 \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, put
--   $$S := W_1[[X_0,X_1]]\big/\big(\,C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v_1 - f_1u_1\,\big),$$
--   with quotient map $\mathrm{mk}_S$, and let $e_1$ be a ring isomorphism from the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic completion of $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ onto $S$. Write $\mathrm{toC}$ for the completion map $\mathcal{O}_{X,z} \to \widehat{\mathcal{O}_{X,z}}$, $\mathrm{germ}_{y'} : C \to \mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ for the canonical map (the germ at $z$ over the open image of `ιFin`, composed with the identifications of global sections of $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$ with $C$), and $\Psi := e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germ}_{y'} : C \to S$. Set $\mathfrak{M} := (\mathrm{mk}_S C(\sigma_1\varpi), \mathrm{mk}_S X_0, \mathrm{mk}_S X_1)$ and $\mathfrak{N} := (\mathrm{mk}_S C(\sigma_1\varpi_t), \mathrm{mk}_S X_0, \mathrm{mk}_S X_1)$ in $S$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hW₁` (seven clauses, summarised here) requires: (i) $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC}$ carries the germ at $z$ of $a \in A$ to the class of the constant $\sigma_1 a$; (ii) every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ with [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — that is, $\tau$ realises on $K$, tested on all quotients $f/g$ of weight-$k$ modular forms for $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions and $g$ with nonzero series, the action of $\gamma^{-1}$ conjugated by $\mathrm{diag}$-scaling at level $q$ (`conjElemN q γ⁻¹`), after any embedding $\iota$ with $\iota(\zeta)=e^{2\pi i/q}$ — maps $C$ into $C$; (iii) if moreover $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$, then the restriction of such a $\tau$ to $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y'$; (iv) for each such $\gamma$ and $\tau$ preserving $C$ and acting trivially modulo $y'$, there exist a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W_1$ and a matrix $M \in M_2(W_1)$ such that $\theta$ intertwines $\Psi$ with the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$, $\theta$ fixes all constants, $\theta(\mathrm{mk}_S X_k) \equiv \sum_i \mathrm{mk}_S(C(M_{ik})X_i)$ modulo $(\mathrm{mk}_S X_0, \mathrm{mk}_S X_1)^2$, $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ and $M_{ik} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ik}$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$, $c \equiv 1$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ whenever $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$, and $c \not\equiv 1$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (v) a separation clause: for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and primes $P_1,P_2$ of $S$, each not containing both $\mathrm{mk}_S X_0$ and $\mathrm{mk}_S X_1$, each containing $\mathrm{mk}_S C(\sigma_1\varpi)$ and a class $\mathrm{mk}_S(C(a_i)X_0 + C(b_i)X_1 + h)$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, if $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ to $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC}$ are distinct; (vi) for a prime $P$ of $S$ not containing both $\mathrm{mk}_S X_0$ and $\mathrm{mk}_S X_1$, containing $\mathrm{mk}_S C(\sigma_1\varpi)$ and a class $\mathrm{mk}_S(C(1)X_0 + C(0)X_1 + h)$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a \in C$ satisfies $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germ}_{y'}(a)) \in e_1^{-1}P$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$; (vii) an existence clause: [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) (the series $\mathrm{jqModC}$ over $L$ with $T$ substituted by $T^{q}$) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and for suitable $a_0 \in A$ with that element congruent to $a_0$ modulo $y'$, a degree $e_0 \ge 1$ and a series $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$, the leading form of $h$ is anisotropic in the sense that $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0} \mathrm{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^i b^{e_0-i}$ is a unit of $W_1$ for all $a,b \in W_1$ with at least one a unit and $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$, while $\Psi$ of that element minus $a_0$ equals $\mathrm{mk}_S h$.
--
--   The centre and the remaining hypotheses. Let $J$ be the ideal of $C$ defined (`hJ`) as the infimum of the ideals $\tau^{-1}\big(\Psi^{-1}\mathfrak{N}\big)$, where $\tau$ runs over the $L$-automorphisms of $K$ preserving $C$ with `IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ` for some $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$. Assume further: (`hcstab`) for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every such $\tau$ preserving $C$ and congruent to the identity modulo $y$, and for every $a \in C$, one has $\Psi(a) \in \mathfrak{N}$ if and only if $\Psi(\tau a) \in \mathfrak{N}$; (`hcen`) $\Psi^{-1}\mathfrak{M} = y$; (`hdense`) for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and every $s \in S$ there is $a \in C$ with $\Psi(a) - s \in \mathfrak{M}^n$; (`hres`) every $c \in C$ is congruent modulo $y$ to the image of an element of $A$; (`hSmax`) $\mathfrak{M}$ is maximal and is the only maximal ideal of $S$.
--
--   Conclusion. Under these hypotheses: the ideal of $S$ generated by $\Psi(J)$ equals $\mathfrak{N}$; there is an ideal $I$ of $C$ with $J = \Psi^{-1}\mathfrak{N} \cap I$ and $I + y = C$; $J \subseteq y$; and the image of $\varpi_t$ under $A \to C$ lies in $J$.
--
--   This is the step identifying the weighted orbit centre $J$ — the intersection of the level-automorphism translates of the contraction of $(\varpi_t, X_0, X_1)$ — as an ideal of the affine chart of the integral model which, after passing to the complete local ring at the supersingular point $z$, generates exactly the Drinfeld chart witness ideal $\mathfrak{N}$, is contained in the point $y$ and contains $\varpi_t$, and differs from the local contraction only by an ideal comaximal with $y$. It is the auxiliary $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-diamond frame of the statement, valid without the restriction $q \ge 5$, and feeds the subsequent analysis of the blow-up chart at $y$: the decomposition of the Drinfeld fibre with its level action, the associated inertia statement, and the description of the primes above the supersingular point on the exceptional and generic components.
